RECTANGLE GUARD, MADE SOUND: one registration, references handed out PER ROW
================================================================================

Host      Apple M4 Pro, 8 P + 4 E cores (12 logical), Darwin 25.5.2.
Base      main ee07b00 (arm `rsbase`).
Arms      rsrect = perf/strided-rect @ c1355c4, the UNSOUND hull-reference form
          rshead = fix/rect-rowview @ this commit
          dav1d_fd1 = dav1d 1.5.4 --framedelay 1, in the SAME interleaved sweep
Build     cargo build --release, default features, NO -C target-cpu=native.
Timing    NO `nice` on any timed run (Darwin maps a niced process onto E-cores
          and the wall clock distorts ~40x). Two-point wall fit at 2 and 20
          frames, beta = (hi - lo)/18, so process startup drops out. Rotating
          arm order inside every cell; strict idle gate (a cell is DISCARDED
          and re-run whenever any foreign process exceeds 25% CPU).
Vectors   ~/tmp/rav1d-perf/vec/v4k_8tile{,_10b}.avif for our arms;
          ~/tmp/recon-yard/vec/*.ivf for dav1d.

Files     rect_rowview_gap_2026-08-09.tsv    raw gap rows (4 arms x 8 cells x N)
          rect_rowview_bands_2026-08-09.txt  per-cell min/median/max + disjoint


0. WHAT WAS WRONG
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
`index_rect_mut` registered the exact rectangle and returned a `&mut [T]` over
the HULL. #469's CHANGELOG stated the split on purpose — "records the hull as
the reference and the exact rectangle as the record" — and that is the defect,
because the REFERENCE is what Rust's aliasing rules bind to, not the record.
Two blocks on the same picture rows in different tile COLUMNS are exactly what
the rectangle record exists to allow, and with a hull reference they are two
simultaneously-live overlapping `&mut`. #470's Miri gate rejected it; its two
controls (per-row guards from two tile columns; one hull guard that is also
RESERVED as the hull) passed, which localises it to the rectangle guard.


1. THE FIX
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
`DisjointMutRect` / `DisjointImmutRect` keep the ONE registration and hand out
one ROW at a time. No reference is created at construction — the guard keeps a
raw base pointer derived from the same `get_mut` the old code used — so every
reference is a subset of what the tracker reserved, and two guards from
different tile columns produce disjoint references. `row_mut` takes `&mut self`,
so two rows of one guard cannot be live at once either. No extra atomic, no
extra lock: the reference shape and the record shape are independent, and only
the record costs a registration.

Converted (already row-indexed, mechanical): `for_rows`, `for_rows_mut`,
`compact_read_per_row`, `compact_write_back_per_row`, `LfBlock::fill_hull`.

NOT converted, and this is the price of the fix rather than an oversight:
`narrow_guard` / `narrow_guard_mut` hand the caller a FLAT slice over the hull
plus a stride. That shape IS a hull reference; narrowing the record does not
narrow it. Both go back to a plain hull borrow, and both callers go back to
gating on `tile_threading_active()`:

  * `with_pixel_guard_immut` — the condition #469 changed sits two lines under
    a doc comment, untouched by that diff, which already said wide guards "are
    unsafe under tile threading even in the immutable case".
  * `block_mut` — loses #469's copy elision under tile threading and takes the
    compact path again. The rectangle's registration win still reaches it one
    level down: the compact copies reserve the block ONCE and copy through
    per-row references, so a block costs 2 registrations instead of `2h`.


