3. WHAT THIS DOES NOT FIX — the macOS-Intel CI panic is a GENUINE overlap
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#469's verification round attributed the intermittent
`Build & Test (macos-15-intel, safe-simd)` failure to the immutable-gate change
handing out a hull, and #455's index note attributed the false-positive class
to the `COL_ANY` degradation. A fresh failure on the merge commit c1355c4
(run 31294761102, job 93197936544) carries a full stack, and it is neither:

    thread 'rav1d-worker-3' panicked at include/dav1d/picture.rs:568:19:
      overlapping DisjointMut:
       current:    & _[241..16385] on ThreadId(22)
      existing: &mut _[16384..20240]
       3: add_wide<false>            tracker_shard.rs:2286
       4: add_rect_immut             tracker_shard.rs:1709
       8: compact_read_per_row       picture.rs:1254
       9: loopfilter_sb_dispatch     src/safe_simd/loopfilter.rs:5134
      14: rav1d_filter_sbrow_deblock_cols

Two facts follow without knowing the plane stride:

  (a) The last byte of a strided rectangle's hull is, by construction, the last
      element of its last ROW — never a gap. A's hull ends at 16385, so A
      covers byte 16384. B covers [16384, 20240). **The two records' covered
      byte sets intersect at 16384. It is a real overlap, not a false positive
      from `COL_ANY` and not an artefact of the hull reference.**
  (b) `src/safe_simd/loopfilter.rs::loopfilter_sb_dispatch` is
      `#[cfg(target_arch = "x86_64")]` (wasm32 shares it); aarch64 dispatches
      to `loopfilter_arm`, which never calls `compact_read_per_row`. That is
      why the verifier's 1,600 aarch64 runs could not reproduce it — **the
      failing call site is not compiled on this box.**

What #469 changed is the WINDOW, not the extent. `main` reads the same tap
window through `h` per-row guards, each live only for its own memcpy; the
rectangle reserves all `h` rows for the whole copy loop. The per-row records
and the rectangle's rows are the SAME bytes — so `main` has the same
unsynchronized overlap and detects it only when the conflict happens to fall
inside the one row whose guard is live at that instant. The rectangle claims
rows before it reads them, which is a stronger claim than the code needs
wherever dav1d's task graph orders the two accesses at finer grain than the
loop.

This is a property of "one registration for a multi-row access", not of the
hull reference, so the row view does not remove it and this branch can still
fail that job. It is named here rather than papered over. Two things follow
for whoever takes it next:

  * The overlapping column is the loop filter's deliberate OVER-READ. The
    8bpc H-luma window is `tap_before + tap_after = 7 + 9 = 16` columns because
    the kernel loads 16 B/row, while the tap ladder only reaches p6..q6 — so
    columns +7 and +8 are read and never used, and byte 16384 is one of them.
    The same file already documents narrowing this window once for exactly this
    reason (the "+16 reached a full 16 rows below a V edge" comment).
  * A cheaper containment is to put `compact_read_per_row` back on per-row
    guards under tile threading, restoring `main`'s window at that ONE site.
    Not done here, because it is a registration-count regression that must be
    priced against a failure rate nobody has measured (1 in 2 macos-15-intel
    runs on 4db4144, 1 in 1 on c1355c4 — n=3 total, and 0 in 1,600 aarch64
    runs at a site that is not compiled there).


4. EXACTNESS OF THE PREDICATE — the claim corrected
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#469's body says the hull+columns test is "EXACT, not conservative". That is
true only between two records that BOTH carry a real column range.
`ShardRecs::c0`'s own doc had it right; the body did not. An interval that runs
off the end of its row registers `0..=COL_ANY` (`RowMap::locate`'s
`c0 + width > stride` arm) and the pair degrades to the plain hull test:
conservative, never missing.

Re-measured here at RECT_ORACLE_ITERS=200000 (8 plane shapes, 1,600,000 pairs,
deterministic seeds — reproduces #470's committed numbers exactly):

    63,020 overlapping / 1,536,980 disjoint pairs
    0 missed overlaps
    2,163 conservative false positives = 0.14% of disjoint pairs,
      EVERY one involving a row-crossing interval

A false positive is a spurious `overlapping DisjointMut`, i.e. a decode
failure, so that is a budget rather than a curiosity. The exact fix is written
down in `ShardRecs::c0` and deliberately NOT taken: a contiguous row-crossing
interval covers `[c0, S)` of its first row, everything between, and `[0, c1]`
of its last, which one `contig` bit per slot plus a three-probe test decides
exactly — but the bit has no free home in the 128-byte record (`SLOTS` already
went 7 -> 5 to fit `c0`/`c1`), and a hand-written three-probe test that is
wrong yields a MISSED overlap, which is the direction that must never regress
for a false-positive win.


